WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.968
A tropical cyclone can be hundreds of

2
00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.327
kilometres wide.

3
00:00:03.327 --> 00:00:05.184
And it starts with something as simple

4
00:00:05.184 --> 00:00:06.773
as warm sea water.

5
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.332
It needs four things.

6
00:00:09.332 --> 00:00:12.699
A sea warmer than about twenty-seven degrees.

7
00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:14.464
Distance from the equator,

8
00:00:14.464 --> 00:00:16.426
so the Earth's spin can twist the

9
00:00:16.426 --> 00:00:17.448
air.

10
00:00:17.448 --> 00:00:18.899
Steady winds high up.

11
00:00:18.899 --> 00:00:20.536
And a patch of low pressure to

12
00:00:20.536 --> 00:00:21.498
begin.

13
00:00:21.898 --> 00:00:23.581
Warm water evaporates.

14
00:00:23.581 --> 00:00:24.812
The moist air rises,

15
00:00:24.812 --> 00:00:26.356
cools and turns into cloud,

16
00:00:26.356 --> 00:00:27.761
releasing heat.

17
00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.839
That heat makes the air rise faster,

18
00:00:29.839 --> 00:00:31.857
and more air rushes in below.

19
00:00:32.257 --> 00:00:34.800
The Earth's spin turns this inflow into

20
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.181
a spiral.

21
00:00:36.181 --> 00:00:37.598
At the centre is the eye:

22
00:00:37.598 --> 00:00:39.304
calm, almost clear,

23
00:00:39.304 --> 00:00:41.243
with the strongest winds in the wall

24
00:00:41.243 --> 00:00:42.309
around it.

25
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:45.195
Around India, the Bay of Bengal sees

26
00:00:45.195 --> 00:00:47.714
roughly four cyclones for every one in

27
00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.014
the Arabian Sea.

28
00:00:49.014 --> 00:00:50.663
The busy months are April to May,

29
00:00:50.663 --> 00:00:52.484
and October to December.

30
00:00:52.883 --> 00:00:55.240
Over land, a cyclone loses its warm

31
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.830
water, its fuel,

32
00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.363
and weakens.

33
00:00:58.363 --> 00:00:59.814
But by then the wind,

34
00:00:59.814 --> 00:01:01.428
rain and storm surge have hit the

35
00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:02.378
coast.

36
00:01:02.779 --> 00:01:04.253
For GS One:

37
00:01:04.253 --> 00:01:06.959
warm sea, rising moist air that releases

38
00:01:06.959 --> 00:01:08.886
heat, the Earth's spin,

39
00:01:08.886 --> 00:01:10.442
and a cyclone that dies once it

40
00:01:10.442 --> 00:01:11.670
reaches land.

